.\" cal.1 -- maux's cal(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered
.\" at build time to build/man/CAL.TXT and staged on the play
.\" image as /usr/man/CAL: `more /usr/man/CAL`.
.TH CAL 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
cal \- display a Gregorian calendar
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cal
[ [
.I month
]
.I year
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no operands,
.I cal
displays a calendar for the current month, read from the
machine's own clock (see
.BR date (1)
for the same clock and its own refusal when none is present).
.PP
With one operand,
.I year
(1\-9999) alone selects that whole year: every month is
printed, one per block, separated by a blank line.  This is a
KNOWN GAP against BSD
.IR cal (1),
whose three\-column full\-year layout this build does not
reproduce.
.PP
With two operands,
.I month
(1\-12) and
.I year
select a single month, rendered to match this project's BSD
reference exactly: a centred title line, the
.B "Su Mo Tu We Th Fr Sa"
header, and six weekly rows (a wholly blank row where the month
needs fewer), every line padded to 22 columns.
.PP
Leap years follow the ordinary Gregorian rule (divisible by 4,
not by 100 unless also by 400).  The day of the week is found
by Sakamoto's algorithm.  The 1752 Julian\-to\-Gregorian
transition is a KNOWN GAP: every year is treated as proleptic
Gregorian.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CAL  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
date(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if no operands were given and the machine
has no valid clock reading; 2 for a usage error (a non\-numeric
operand, a month outside 1\-12, a year outside 1\-9999, or more
than two operands).
.SH BUGS
No three\-column full\-year layout (see above).  No support for
the pre\-1752 Julian calendar.
